situation, not just understanding a joke.\n*   **D) indicates no progress made:** This aligns perfectly with the contradiction. If they are "almost done" but haven't even "started," it means zero progress has been made toward the actual goal, making the preceding claims of being "out of things to say" or "almost done" ironic.\n\n**Conclusion:** The sarcasm lies in the juxtaposition of being "almost done" with the necessity of having to "start," highlighting that no meaningful progress has occurred.\n\nThe best fit remains D.\n\nAnswer: $\boxed{D}$
